specs/system-speckit/054-weak-model-spec-authoring/002-template-and-example-defect-repair/tasks.md:319:- [ ] T010 Fix the `NEEDS-WORKER` rows with one Task subagent each (`scratch/briefs/T010-<id>.txt`, `scratch/runs/T010-<id>.txt` and the one file each row names). Skip this task when T009 printed no `NEEDS-WORKER` line and when T001 reported a missing ledger. A row lands here when its locator no longer occurs exactly once, which means the defect is prose that needs an edit instead of a pure swap. The text may also have drifted. Before the first dispatch, run `rg -c 'deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017' opencode.json`, which must print a number above 0, because a Task subagent inherits the session model. Then read `.skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/SKILL.md` sections 3 and 4, whose NEVER rules 1 and 5 forbid a CLI worker dispatch from inside OpenCode. Run the prepare block below. It writes one brief per row that still needs a worker and prints those row ids. It skips a row that is already fixed, so running it again prepares briefs only for the rows that still failed. For each printed id, dispatch with the Task tool: subagent_type `general`, description `T010-<id>`, prompt = the full text of `$PHASE/scratch/briefs/T010-<id>.txt`. When the subagent returns, write its final reply verbatim to `$PHASE/scratch/runs/T010-<id>.txt` with the write tool. At most 3 Task calls in one message, and never two that write the same file. When every call has returned, run the check block. When the prepare block printed no id, the check block prints nothing and the task is already met. Run the whole task at most three times in all. Verify: every printed line ends in `repro=NO extra=0 reply=present`. A row still failing after the third run is marked `[B]` under the blocked task protocol in `plan.md`.
specs/system-speckit/054-weak-model-spec-authoring/002-template-and-example-defect-repair/tasks.md:352:- [ ] T011 Delete other dead files named by the census (the files the rows name). Skip this task when T009 printed no `DELETE-PENDING` line. The block below runs the same reader guard as T008 with each file's base name and deletes the file only when ripgrep exits with code `1`, which means no reader. Any other code leaves the file and prints `KEPT`. A base name that other files mention, such as `README.md`, makes ripgrep exit with code `0`, so that file is also kept. Record every kept file in `goal.md` section 4. Verify: the two commands under the block print `0` and `0`. The first counts lines that do not start with `DELETED` or `KEPT`. The second counts `DELETED` paths that still exist.
